CASES={}
def cases(module,text):
 rows=[]
 for line in text.strip().splitlines():
  x=[v.strip() for v in line.split('|')];assert len(x)==9,(module,line);rows.append(x)
 CASES[module]=rows
# topic | stem | correct | three distractors | correct reasoning | distractor reasoning | linked concept
cases(1, '''
Reference posture | A seated student turns the palms backward. Which statement about anatomical terminology remains correct? | Directions still use the standard anatomical reference | The thumb must now be named medial in all descriptions | Anterior now means whichever surface faces the examiner | Proximal now means closest to the camera | Position can change without redefining the standard reference used for anatomical terms. | The alternatives substitute current appearance or observer location for the specified anatomical reference. | Anatomical position
Right and left | A clinician faces a patient. The clinician's left hand points to the patient's right cheek. Which side should be recorded? | Patient's right | Patient's left | Observer's left as the anatomical side | Either side because the observer faces the patient | Clinical side labels refer to the person being described. | Observer-relative labels can reverse the patient's anatomical side and should not replace it. | Subject's right and left
Anterior-posterior relation | In the cervical region the oesophagus lies behind the trachea. Which statement expresses this relation correctly? | Oesophagus is posterior to trachea | Oesophagus is anterior to trachea | Trachea is posterior to oesophagus | Oesophagus is distal to trachea by definition | Posterior describes position toward the back in the standard reference. | The first two spatial reversals contradict the stated relation; distal requires an appropriate origin/attachment reference. | Posterior
Medial-lateral relation | A coronal nasal image shows a concha beside the septum. Which relation is most appropriate? | Concha is lateral to the septum | Concha is medial to the septum | Septum is lateral to both conchae | Concha is proximal because it is visible | The septum is the midline partition; lateral walls and conchae lie away from it. | The reversed relations misplace the midline, and visibility does not define proximal. | Lateral
Proximal-distal | Which statement correctly compares the elbow and wrist? | Elbow is proximal to wrist | Elbow is distal to wrist | Wrist is proximal to elbow | Their relation depends only on which is nearer the examiner | Proximal means nearer the limb's attachment; the elbow is nearer than the wrist. | Distal is the opposite relationship, and examiner distance is irrelevant. | Proximal
Depth | Skin overlies the masseter from a lateral approach. Which description is correct? | Masseter is deep to skin | Masseter is superficial to skin | Skin is distal to masseter by definition | Skin must be posterior to masseter | Deep-superficial concerns distance from the relevant surface. | The first alternative reverses depth; distal and posterior do not follow from an overlying relation alone. | Deep
Section plane | A vertical section separates right and left portions of the head. Which plane is described? | Sagittal | Coronal | Transverse | A plane defined only by the examiner's height | Sagittal planes separate right and left portions; only the midline one is median. | Coronal separates front/back and transverse upper/lower; examiner height does not define the plane. | Sagittal plane
Median distinction | Which feature distinguishes a median plane from other sagittal planes? | It passes through the midline | It must be horizontal | It separates anterior from posterior | It can only be used for the arm | The median plane is the midline member of the sagittal family. | Horizontal and anterior-posterior divisions describe other orientations, and the concept is not arm-specific. | Median plane
Flexion | During a demonstration, the elbow angle decreases as a cup approaches the mouth. Which movement is illustrated? | Flexion | Abduction | Supination | Retraction | The stated change is a decrease in the angle at the elbow. | The alternatives concern movement away from an axis, forearm rotation or posterior displacement. | Flexion
Ankle movement | A student brings the toes toward the shin without turning the sole inward or outward. Which movement is central? | Dorsiflexion | Plantarflexion | Inversion | Eversion | Dorsiflexion brings the dorsum toward the anterior leg. | Plantarflexion points away; inversion and eversion change sole orientation. | Dorsiflexion
Digit reference | Finger abduction is best assessed relative to which reference in the usual teaching convention? | The middle finger's longitudinal axis | The thumb as the axis for every finger | The observer's body midline | The direction of gravity | Digit movements use specified local axes rather than simply trunk position. | The other references do not define the standard finger-abduction convention. | Abduction
Circular movement | An arm's distal end traces a circle while its segment describes a cone. Which term fits best? | Circumduction | Pure axial rotation only | Forearm pronation only | Scapular elevation only | Circumduction combines angular movements to create a conical pattern. | A circular distal path is not identical to pure long-axis rotation or the other isolated actions. | Circumduction
Axial movement | A bone turns around its own long axis without requiring a conical distal path. Which term is most precise? | Rotation | Circumduction | Protraction | Gliding | Long-axis turning defines rotation. | Circumduction traces a cone; protraction and gliding describe translational components. | Rotation
Forearm rotation | With the elbow flexed, the palm turns upward to support a bowl. Which movement is most directly illustrated? | Supination | Pronation | Wrist flexion alone | Shoulder adduction alone | Supination provides a palm-up example in this elbow position. | Pronation reverses it, and wrist/shoulder changes do not define the radioulnar rotation. | Supination
Radius-ulna relationship | Which finding is characteristic of forearm pronation? | Radius crosses over ulna | Radius and ulna become one fused bone | The sole turns medially | The mandible moves upward | The relationship of the forearm bones changes during pronation. | Fusion is not normal movement, and the other observations concern different body regions. | Pronation
Foot orientation | A demonstrator turns the sole medially. Which term is appropriate? | Inversion | Eversion | Dorsiflexion | Plantarflexion | Inversion describes the sole turning inward. | Eversion turns it outward; dorsiflexion and plantarflexion address a different component. | Inversion
Jaw translation | The mandible moves forward without the question specifying closure. Which movement is named? | Protraction | Retraction | Elevation | Depression | Protraction identifies anterior displacement. | Retraction is posterior; elevation and depression describe vertical displacement. | Protraction
Jaw closure | The lower teeth move upward toward the upper teeth. Which component is described? | Elevation | Depression | Protraction | Retraction | Superior mandibular movement is elevation. | The alternatives describe inferior or anterior-posterior components not specified here. | Elevation
Movement qualification | The lecture lists gliding at the sternoclavicular joint. Which inference should be avoided? | The joint must therefore be a simple plane joint | Gliding can occur as a component of complex joint movement | A joint's movement and structural classification are distinguishable | Movement descriptions need the relevant joint identified | A movement component does not alone establish a structural joint class; sternoclavicular is classically a saddle-type synovial joint. | The other statements preserve the distinction between movement and classification. | Gliding
Reference integration | Which pair correctly describes structures on the same side and on opposite sides, respectively? | Ipsilateral; contralateral | Contralateral; ipsilateral | Medial; proximal | Deep; superficial | These terms describe side relationships rather than position along another axis. | The reversed pair swaps the definitions; the other pairs address different dimensions. | Ipsilateral
''')
cases(2, '''
Anterior and posterior openings | Air leaves a nasal cavity posteriorly to enter the nasopharynx. Which opening is traversed? | Choana | Anterior naris | Nasolacrimal ostium | Oesophageal hiatus | Choanae are the posterior nasal apertures. | The naris is anterior; the duct carries tears; the hiatus belongs to the diaphragm. | Choanae
Hard-palate relation | Which structure is both the nasal cavity floor and the oral cavity roof? | Hard palate | Cribriform plate | Soft-palate free edge | Sphenoid sinus roof | The bony hard palate separates the two cavities. | Cribriform is nasal roof, the soft palate is mobile/posterior, and sphenoid roof does not form the oral roof. | Nasal cavity floor
Roof and smell | Olfactory filaments pass through which structure toward the cranial cavity? | Cribriform plate | Vomer | Inferior concha | Horizontal palatine plate | The perforated ethmoid plate transmits CN I filaments. | The alternatives belong to septal, lateral-wall or floor anatomy rather than this route. | Cribriform plate
Septal components | Which pairing best identifies the major superior and posteroinferior bony septal components? | Perpendicular ethmoid plate; vomer | Inferior concha; zygomatic arch | Nasal tip cartilage; mandibular condyle | Cribriform plate; coronoid process | The septal vertical ethmoid plate and vomer form important bony portions. | The alternatives mix different walls or entirely different regions and confuse horizontal with vertical ethmoid parts. | Perpendicular plate of ethmoid
Concha distinction | Which main nasal concha is an independent bone? | Inferior | Middle | Superior | All three are independent bones | The inferior concha is separate, whereas middle and superior conchae are ethmoidal parts. | The other choices misclassify ethmoidal projections or all three together. | Inferior nasal concha
Tear drainage | Increased tears can reach which nasal passage through the nasolacrimal duct? | Inferior meatus | Middle meatus | Superior meatus | Sphenoethmoidal recess | The duct opens beneath the inferior concha. | The other passages are associated with different principal sinus openings. | Inferior meatus drainage
Drainage grouping | Frontal, maxillary and anterior/middle ethmoidal drainage is chiefly associated with which passage? | Middle meatus | Inferior meatus | Superior meatus exclusively | Pharyngoesophageal segment | The middle-meatus region receives these major pathways. | Inferior receives the nasolacrimal duct; superior receives posterior ethmoidal cells; the final option is not nasal. | Middle meatus drainage
Posterior ethmoid | Where do posterior ethmoidal cells principally drain? | Superior meatus | Inferior meatus | Oral vestibule | Maxillary alveolus | Posterior ethmoidal drainage is associated with the superior meatus. | The other sites do not provide its normal principal route. | Superior meatus drainage
Sphenoid opening | The natural sphenoid sinus opening is associated with which space? | Sphenoethmoidal recess | Inferior meatus | Oral vestibule | External acoustic meatus | The recess lies above/posterior to the superior concha. | The alternatives are different passages and do not represent the natural sphenoid opening. | Sphenoethmoidal recess
Modality and nerve | A question asks about nasal irritation rather than smell. Which major cranial system should be considered first? | Trigeminal V1/V2 sensory pathways | Olfactory CN I exclusively | Hypoglossal CN XII motor pathway | Vestibulocochlear CN VIII | Ordinary sensation differs from special olfaction. | CN I concerns smell, XII motor tongue supply and VIII hearing/balance; none replaces general nasal sensory pathways. | Nasal general sensation
Carotid routes | Sphenopalatine and anterior ethmoidal arteries belong principally to which parent systems, respectively? | External carotid and internal carotid | Internal carotid and external carotid | Vertebral and coronary | Pulmonary and hepatic | Sphenopalatine follows maxillary/ECA; anterior ethmoidal follows ophthalmic/ICA. | The reversed pair swaps them, while the other systems are anatomically unrelated to the principal routes. | Sphenopalatine artery
Sinus clearance | The maxillary ostium lies relatively high. What best explains normal mucus transport toward it? | Coordinated mucociliary transport | Gravity as the sole mechanism | Mandatory forceful nose blowing for every clearance cycle | Contraction of the masseter inside the sinus | Cilia move secretions toward the natural opening. | Gravity alone is incomplete, forceful blowing is not a physiological requirement, and masseter is not an intrasinus pump. | Mucociliary sinus clearance
Dental relation | Which sinus has important inferior relationships with roots of upper posterior teeth? | Maxillary | Frontal | Sphenoid | Posterior ethmoidal cells only | The maxillary sinus lies in the maxilla above upper posterior tooth regions. | The other sinuses do not have the same direct inferior dental relationship. | Maxillary sinus
Orbit relation | A thin barrier between ethmoidal cells and orbit helps explain which potential complication? | Spread of infection toward orbital tissues | Direct passage of every swallowed bolus into the orbit | Mandatory paralysis of all tongue muscles | Loss of the mandibular condyle | Proximity and a thin wall provide a mechanism for potential spread. | The alternatives invoke unrelated pathways or inevitable effects not supported by the anatomy. | Ethmoid-orbit relationship
Frontal variation | A scan shows unequal frontal sinus sizes. Which conclusion is most appropriate from that finding alone? | Asymmetry can be a normal anatomical variation | Every asymmetry proves a fracture | Both sinuses must normally be identical | One side must be a maxillary sinus | Frontal sinus development is variable and often asymmetric. | The image alone does not establish trauma or change the identity of a sinus. | Frontal sinus
Phonation and resonance | Which statement best separates vocal sound generation from nasal/sinus acoustic influence? | Vocal folds generate voiced sound; upper cavities modify its acoustic characteristics | Sinuses replace vocal folds as the primary source of voiced sound | A normal sinus must vibrate like a muscular vocal fold | Every resonance difference is evidence of sinus infection | Source and filter functions are related but distinct. | The alternatives assign an incorrect primary source or infer disease from an acoustic observation alone. | Sinus functions
Nasal roof trauma | Clear nasal fluid after significant head trauma raises concern about injury near which barrier? | Anterior skull base/cribriform region | Mandibular coronoid process | Inferior oesophageal sphincter | Zygomatic arch alone | Skull-base communication can permit CSF leakage and requires professional assessment. | The other structures do not form the relevant nasal-cranial barrier; clear fluid alone still does not confirm diagnosis. | Cribriform injury
Tonsillar locations | Which pair correctly identifies adenoids and palatine tonsils? | Nasopharyngeal roof region; lateral oropharynx | Oral floor; frontal sinus | Inferior meatus; oesophagus | Middle ear; tongue tip | The two named tonsillar tissues occupy different pharyngeal regions. | The other pairs misplace both structures. | Waldeyer's ring
Tubal anatomy | The tubal tonsil is most closely associated with which structure? | Pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube | Lower oesophageal sphincter | Mandibular foramen | Maxillary sinus floor | It is associated with the tubal region of the lateral nasopharyngeal wall. | The other sites are different anatomical regions; the adjacent pharyngeal recess is not a synonym for the tonsil. | Tubal tonsil
Regional lymph | Which statement preserves the difference between nasal blood supply and lymph drainage? | Arterial branches and regional nodes belong to distinct systems | A facial artery branch is itself a lymph node | The nasal vestibule drains only into pulmonary arteries | Venous plexus and deep cervical node are identical structures | Vessels and lymphatic nodes must be traced through their respective systems. | The alternatives conflate arterial, venous and lymphatic structures. | Nasal lymph drainage
''')
cases(3, '''
Bleeding location | Which site is most characteristically associated with a common anterior septal nosebleed? | Little's area | Sphenoethmoidal recess | Oesophageal hiatus | Palatine tonsillar fossa | Little's area is the anterior septal region associated with Kiesselbach's anastomosis. | The other sites are different nasal or nonnasal structures, not the classic anterior septal area. | Little's area
Plexus identity | What best describes Kiesselbach's plexus? | An arterial anastomotic region on the anterior septum | A single motor nerve to nasal cartilage | A posterior tongue lymph node | A purely venous structure in the oesophagus | The important features are anterior septal location and arterial contributions from several routes. | Nerves, lymph nodes and a distant venous structure do not describe the named nasal anastomosis. | Kiesselbach's plexus
Contributing vessel | A septal branch of the superior labial artery traces back chiefly through which named artery? | Facial | Ophthalmic | Lingual | Ascending pharyngeal | Superior labial is a facial branch. | The alternatives represent other regional arterial routes rather than this parent branch. | Superior labial septal branch
Posterior-source qualification | Blood is seen in the throat during a nosebleed. Which conclusion is justified? | A posterior source is possible, but the site still needs evaluation | The exact posterior vessel is identified by that sign alone | All anterior sources are excluded | No airway or circulatory assessment can be relevant | Blood can track backward from different nasal sites; assessment determines source and severity. | The alternatives over-localize, falsely exclude anterior blood tracking or disregard clinical priorities. | Posterior epistaxis
Woodruff distinction | Which correction is most appropriate to a slide equating Woodruff's plexus with every posterior artery? | The classic label is venous; posterior epistaxis often involves arterial branches | Woodruff and Kiesselbach are the same anterior site | Posterior bleeding cannot involve sphenopalatine branches | All nasal bleeding must arise from one vein | Historical venous terminology does not replace the arterial anatomy of many posterior bleeds. | The other claims misplace the plexus or exclude relevant arterial sources. | Woodruff's plexus
Local versus systemic | A person taking an anticoagulant injures anterior nasal mucosa. Which interpretation is most appropriate? | Local injury and altered hemostasis may both contribute | A local trigger excludes a medication contribution | Medication use proves that no mucosal injury exists | Only one category can be recorded in a bleeding history | A trigger and a systemic/medication modifier can coexist. | The alternatives impose a false choice between mechanisms rather than integrating them. | Traumatic epistaxis
Dryness and injury | Why can dry mucosa predispose to recurrent local bleeding? | Crusting and irritation can make local tissue vulnerable | Dryness directly proves an inherited factor deficiency | Dry mucosa eliminates all arterial anastomoses | Dryness transforms a concha into a septal perforation in every case | The mechanism is local tissue vulnerability, not a sufficient diagnosis of a systemic disorder. | The alternatives make unsupported mechanistic or inevitable structural claims. | Mucosal dryness
Age association | A slide links older age with hypertension. What should an exam explanation avoid? | Diagnosing the cause from age alone | Considering age as one contextual factor | Taking a relevant history | Assessing the bleeding site and severity | Demographic associations do not identify a cause in an individual. | The other actions preserve context and clinical evidence rather than replacing them with an age rule. | Neoplasm as a differential
Blood-pressure inference | A high reading occurs during a stressful nosebleed. Which inference is defensible? | The reading needs clinical interpretation and does not prove sole causation | Pressure alone is proven as the cause by one measurement | Local bleeding sources are thereby excluded | The individual necessarily has HHT | Elevated pressure can coexist with stress and other causes; association is not proof of a single mechanism. | Neither exclusion of local disease nor a specific vascular diagnosis follows from the reading. | Hypertension and epistaxis
Vascular disorder | Recurrent epistaxis, characteristic telangiectasias and family history raise which educational differential? | Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia | Isolated temporalis weakness | A normal maxillary ostium | Pure hypoglossal neuropathy | The features suggest considering a hereditary vascular condition using appropriate clinical criteria. | The alternatives do not explain this combination of recurrent bleeding and vascular/familial findings. | Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia
Hemostasis mechanisms | Which comparison correctly separates two bleeding mechanisms? | Thrombocytopenia affects platelet number; hemophilia concerns clotting-factor pathways | Both are identical reductions of the same platelet count | Both are names for a deviated septum | Neither can influence bleeding beyond the nasal cartilage | Platelet and coagulation-factor disorders are distinguishable mechanisms. | The alternatives conflate mechanisms or incorrectly restrict their effects. | Platelet versus coagulation problems
Terminology update | Which modern name corresponds to the condition formerly called Wegener's granulomatosis? | Granulomatosis with polyangiitis | Ordinary allergic rhinitis | Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia | Isolated septal deviation | GPA is the current standard name for this systemic vasculitic disorder. | The alternatives are different conditions and cannot substitute simply because nasal symptoms can occur. | Granulomatosis with polyangiitis
Urgent priorities | In a teaching case, nasal bleeding is heavy and accompanied by faintness. Which principle takes priority? | Prompt assessment of airway, circulation and severity | Waiting seventy-two hours because a slide lists that interval | Assuming all bleeds are minor because anterior bleeding is common | Choosing a surgical artery before assessing the person | Severity and systemic effects govern urgency and initial evaluation. | A fixed wait, population frequency or premature procedural choice cannot replace assessment. | Initial epistaxis priorities
Compression principle | Which first-aid description matches commonly recommended posture and pressure location? | Lean forward and compress the soft lower nose | Tilt the head back and pinch only the bony bridge | Lie flat and repeatedly release pressure to check every few seconds | Insert a posterior pack without training | Forward posture and sustained soft-part pressure are standard principles; seek care for persistent/heavy bleeding or concerning symptoms. | The alternatives can misdirect pressure, interrupt control, encourage backward blood flow or substitute an invasive procedure. | Nosebleed first-aid principle
Cautery recognition | Silver nitrate in the lecture belongs to which treatment category? | Clinician-applied cautery of a suitable identified bleeding site | Routine measurement of nasal airflow | A replacement name for the nasopalatine nerve | A mandatory home procedure for every nosebleed | The category is professional treatment directed at an appropriate site. | It is not a diagnostic nerve or airflow label and is not a universal self-treatment instruction. | Nasal cautery
Packing limitation | Why might bleeding continue despite an anterior pack? | The site or mechanism may not be adequately controlled by that intervention | An anterior pack guarantees posterior control, so ongoing blood cannot be nasal | All continued bleeding proves a specific malignancy | Continued bleeding excludes any need for reassessment | Response depends on site, severity and several clinical factors. | A pack is not universally effective, and persistence does not alone diagnose a particular cause. | Anterior nasal packing
Monitoring | Why can posterior packing require hospital monitoring? | Airway and cardiorespiratory concerns may accompany the condition and intervention | A posterior pack is always equivalent to a harmless external dressing | Every patient must receive the identical antibiotic regimen | Monitoring is needed only to count tongue papillae | Clinicians assess the person's condition and procedure-related risks. | The alternatives trivialize the intervention, impose a blanket medication rule or identify an unrelated target. | Posterior packing and monitoring
Persistent bleeding | Which statement best describes escalation after unsuccessful initial measures? | Specialist assessment may lead to targeted endoscopic, surgical or embolization approaches | All people must wait the same fixed duration irrespective of severity | One failed measure makes vascular anatomy irrelevant | Medication history should be discarded before further care | Anatomy, clinical condition and treatment response guide escalation. | Fixed delays, abandonment of localization and ignoring history are not sound general principles. | Escalation for persistent bleeding
Septal defect | An image shows an opening connecting the two nasal sides through the septum. Which term identifies the structure change? | Septal perforation | Septal deviation only | Inferior concha hypertrophy by definition | Normal choana | Perforation means a through-defect; deviation means displacement rather than necessarily a hole. | The alternatives describe another shape change, a lateral-wall feature or a normal posterior aperture. | Septal perforation
Prevention rationale | Why is avoiding renewed picking or forceful blowing often discussed after control of a nosebleed? | Mechanical disturbance can reinjure vulnerable tissue or disturb the controlled site | All nasal mucus must remain permanently uncleared | The advice proves the original bleed was malignant | It replaces every individualized follow-up instruction | Prevention addresses renewed local trauma. | It does not require permanent blockage, establish a diagnosis or remove individualized clinical advice. | Prevention and follow-up
''')
cases(4, '''
Joint identification | A joint lies anterior to the external acoustic meatus and links the mandibular condyle to the temporal region. Which joint is described? | Temporomandibular | Atlantoaxial | Sternoclavicular | Glenohumeral | The mandibular-temporal articulation is the TMJ. | The alternatives involve cervical vertebrae, clavicle-sternum or shoulder rather than mandible-temporal articulation. | Temporomandibular joint
Articular process | Which mandibular process forms the principal articular head at the TMJ? | Condylar process/head | Coronoid process | Alveolar process alone | Mental spine | The condyle participates in the articulation; temporalis attaches to the coronoid region. | The alternatives are attachment or tooth-bearing regions rather than the articular head. | TMJ articular surfaces
Functional classification | Which description best captures TMJ movement? | Hinge-like rotation plus translation | Pure hinge action without translation | A fixed fibrous union without movement | Pure axial rotation of the radius | The TMJ is a modified hinge/gliding functional system. | The alternatives omit translation, deny synovial movement or describe another limb movement. | Modified hinge classification
Articular covering | Which covering is the conventional teaching distinction for TMJ articular surfaces? | Fibrous/fibrocartilaginous tissue | Typical hyaline cartilage as an obligatory universal rule | Tooth enamel | Respiratory epithelium | The TMJ differs from many synovial joints in its articular covering. | Enamel and respiratory epithelium are different tissues, and a universal hyaline rule is incorrect. | TMJ surface covering
Disc compartments | Where does hinge-like rotation chiefly occur in the introductory compartment model? | Inferior compartment between condyle and disc | Superior compartment alone | Nasal inferior meatus | Between tongue and hard palate | The inferior TMJ compartment is associated mainly with rotation. | Translation predominates above the disc; the remaining spaces are not TMJ compartments. | Inferior TMJ compartment
Translation | During wider mouth opening, the disc-condyle complex translates relative to which region? | Temporal articular eminence in the superior compartment | Tongue frenulum in the inferior oral cavity | Coronoid tip inside the maxillary sinus | Hyoid greater horn within the nasal septum | Translation of the complex along the temporal articular region uses the superior joint compartment. | The alternatives misplace the moving complex and relevant articular surfaces. | Superior TMJ compartment
Disc form | Which description best fits the TMJ disc in a sagittal teaching view? | Generally biconcave with a thinner intermediate region | A solid spherical tooth crown | A rigid bony septum separating the nostrils | A ring of laryngeal cartilage | The disc is a fibrous/fibrocartilaginous structure adapted to changing articular relationships. | The alternatives describe different tissues or organs. | Articular disc
Ligament attachments | A band extends from the sphenoid spine to the mandibular lingula. Which ligament is described? | Sphenomandibular | Stylomandibular | Lateral collateral ligament of the knee | Median lingual septum | Its specified attachments identify the sphenomandibular ligament. | Stylomandibular starts at the styloid region, while the other structures belong to different sites. | Sphenomandibular ligament
Joint sensation | Which cranial division is the main parent pathway for auriculotemporal sensory supply to the TMJ? | V3 | V1 | VII | XII | Auriculotemporal is a mandibular trigeminal branch. | V1 is ophthalmic, VII is not the principal joint sensory parent here, and XII mainly supplies tongue motor function. | Auriculotemporal nerve
Masticatory motor supply | Which division supplies the four principal muscles of mastication? | Mandibular division of V | Ophthalmic division of V | Facial nerve VII | Hypoglossal XII | V3 motor branches supply masseter, temporalis and both pterygoids. | The alternatives represent different sensory or motor systems. | Mastication motor supply
Muscle attachment | A broad muscle runs from the zygomatic arch to the lateral mandibular ramus/angle. Which muscle is described? | Masseter | Temporalis | Medial pterygoid | Styloglossus | The lateral attachment pattern and zygomatic origin identify masseter. | Temporalis attaches at coronoid, medial pterygoid on the medial angle, and styloglossus to tongue. | Masseter attachments
Retraction | Which muscle component is particularly important in bringing a protruded jaw backward? | Posterior temporalis fibers | Intrinsic vertical tongue fibers | Nasal concha mucosa | Chorda tympani fibers | Posterior temporalis orientation supports retrusion. | The alternatives concern tongue shape, nasal lining or nerve fibers rather than a jaw-retracting muscle component. | Temporalis action
Pterygoid position | Which muscle inserts on the medial ramus/angle and contributes to the sling with masseter? | Medial pterygoid | Lateral pterygoid alone | Superior longitudinal tongue muscle | Buccinator as one of the principal four | The medial pterygoid's position complements masseter on the lateral side. | Lateral pterygoid targets the condylar/disc region; the others do not form this principal masticatory sling. | Medial pterygoid attachments
Opening mechanism | Which account best explains mouth opening? | Coordinated rotation, translation and muscular assistance | Contraction of one isolated muscle irrespective of hyoid position | Movement of the upper jaw alone | A rigid disc remaining fixed while all other structures fuse | Jaw opening is a coordinated movement with context-dependent suprahyoid contribution. | The alternatives exclude necessary coordination or describe an incorrect mechanism. | Mandibular depression
Stable attachment | Why does hyoid stabilization matter when suprahyoid muscles assist jaw depression? | Muscle effect depends on which attachment is stabilized | Stabilization converts nerves into arteries | All suprahyoids have identical effects regardless of fixation | The hyoid is part of the temporal articular disc | A muscle can move different attached structures depending on fixation. | The alternatives confuse structures or deny a basic mechanical relationship. | Suprahyoid assistance
Lateral movement | In a simplified model, stronger left pterygoid activity moves the jaw chiefly toward which side? | Right | Left as a universal rule | Superior only with no lateral component | Neither side because pterygoids cannot contribute | Unilateral pterygoid action generally contributes to contralateral excursion. | The alternatives reverse the simplified relationship or deny the contribution; actual movement still depends on coordination. | Unilateral pterygoid action
Peripheral weakness | Assume isolated right peripheral V3 masticatory weakness. Which opening pattern is expected in the teaching model? | Deviation toward the right weak side | Deviation always left regardless of lesion type | No possible relation between weakness and movement | Tongue taste loss as the defining jaw movement | Unopposed contralateral pterygoid contribution can deviate the jaw toward the weak side. | The rule requires the stated motor context and does not equate jaw movement with taste. | Mandibular nerve weakness
Wide opening | After excessive opening, a condyle moves beyond the articular eminence and closure is impaired. What anatomical mechanism is illustrated? | Anterior TMJ dislocation | Ordinary taste adaptation | Posterior septal perforation | Oesophageal peristalsis | The description places the condyle anterior to its usual restraining articular relationship. | The other events occur in unrelated structures; this is an educational mechanism, not a reduction instruction. | TMJ dislocation principle
Blood-supply table | Which artery pairing is appropriate for temporalis and masseter, respectively? | Deep temporal; masseteric | Lingual; anterior ethmoidal | Coronary; pulmonary | Superior labial; dorsal nasal | Both named branches arise in the maxillary-system muscular supply context. | The alternatives pair unrelated regional or systemic vessels with these muscles. | Deep temporal arteries
Chewing and phonation | Which statement correctly distinguishes TMJ support from voice production? | Jaw motion positions articulators; vocal folds provide the principal voiced source | The TMJ disc replaces vocal folds during vowels | Every jaw click is voiced phonation | Articulatory position has no relationship to speech | Jaw function supports articulation without being the normal voiced sound generator. | The alternatives conflate joint noise and laryngeal phonation or deny articulation. | Mastication and speech
''')
cases(5, '''
Regional extent | A passage extends from the skull base toward the lower cricoid border and lies behind nose, mouth and larynx. Which structure is described? | Pharynx | Trachea | Maxillary sinus | Mandibular canal | This is the fibromuscular pharyngeal passage and its usual adult extent. | The alternatives have different courses, boundaries and functions. | Pharynx
Layer order | Which sequence correctly proceeds outward from the pharyngeal lumen in the teaching model? | Mucosa, pharyngobasilar fascia, muscle, buccopharyngeal fascia | Muscle, mucosa, buccopharyngeal fascia, nasal bone | Buccopharyngeal fascia, enamel, mucosa, muscle | Pharyngobasilar fascia, taste bud, condyle, mucosa | The sequence separates luminal lining, supporting fascia, muscle and outer covering. | The alternatives reverse the relationship or introduce tissues unrelated to this wall sequence. | Pharyngeal wall layers
Nasopharyngeal localization | Which pair belongs most directly to nasopharyngeal anatomy? | Pharyngeal tonsil and auditory-tube opening | Lingual frenulum and mandibular condyle | Lower oesophageal sphincter and gastric cardia | Inferior concha and oesophageal hiatus as identical structures | Adenoid tissue and the tubal opening are characteristic landmarks behind the nasal cavities. | The alternatives mix oral, joint, gastric or incorrectly equated structures. | Nasopharynx
Tonsillar location | A palatine tonsil is located between which arches? | Palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal | Zygomatic and aortic | Superior and inferior longitudinal tongue fibers | Ethmoid and sphenoid sinus ostia | These arches form the anterior and posterior faucial pillars. | The alternatives do not form the tonsillar pillars. | Palatine tonsil
Anterior pillar | Which muscle is associated with the anterior faucial pillar? | Palatoglossus | Stylopharyngeus | Masseter | Genioglossus | The anterior arch is palatoglossal. | The other muscles have different attachments and do not define this pillar. | Palatoglossal arch
Lower pharyngeal space | A diagram shows paired recesses beside the laryngeal inlet. What are they? | Pyriform recesses | Frontal sinuses | Nasal vestibules | Submandibular ducts | Pyriform recesses are lateral hypopharyngeal channels. | The other structures lie in different regions and do not flank the laryngeal inlet. | Pyriform recess
Motor exception | Which muscle is the major IX motor exception to the usual vagal pharyngeal pattern? | Stylopharyngeus | Superior constrictor | Middle constrictor | Palatopharyngeus | Stylopharyngeus receives glossopharyngeal motor supply. | The alternatives follow the usual vagal pharyngeal motor pattern in the introductory map. | Stylopharyngeus
Palatal exception | Which pathway supplies tensor veli palatini? | V3 motor branch | CN I | Chorda tympani taste fibers | CN XII as the usual palate rule | Tensor veli palatini is the commonly tested mandibular-division palatal exception. | The alternatives serve olfaction, taste/secretomotor routes or most tongue muscles rather than this palatal muscle. | Tensor veli palatini
Sensory sequence | Which regional general-sensory sequence fits nasopharynx, oropharynx and laryngopharynx? | V2, IX, X | XII, VII, I | VIII, V1, XII | IX, I, VII | The teaching regional map assigns those principal sensory pathways in order. | The other sequences conflate different modalities and territories. | Pharyngeal sensory map
Reflex interpretation | A person has a gag response during examination. Which conclusion should NOT follow from that alone? | Swallowing is guaranteed safe in every condition | The observation gives some information about a reflex pathway | Other swallowing evidence may still be needed | Reflex and functional swallowing assessments answer different questions | A gag response is not an independent guarantee against aspiration. | The other statements retain the limited role of one observation without overclaiming safety. | Gag reflex pathways
Arterial parents | Ascending palatine and ascending pharyngeal arteries arise chiefly from which named parents, respectively? | Facial and external carotid | Ophthalmic and lingual | Coronary and pulmonary | Maxillary and facial as an obligatory identical pair | Similar names conceal different parent branches. | The alternatives assign incorrect parents or conflate similarly named arteries. | Ascending pharyngeal artery
Descending palatine | Which parent artery provides the descending palatine pathway in the handout? | Maxillary | Facial | Vertebral | Internal thoracic | The descending palatine route belongs to the maxillary arterial system. | The alternatives are different arterial origins. | Descending palatine pathway
Lymph distinction | Nasopharyngeal lymph commonly reaches which regional pathways? | Retropharyngeal and upper deep cervical nodes | Coronary arteries | Axillary nodes exclusively | Lingual motor axons | Lymphatic drainage is regional and distinct from arterial or nerve supply. | The alternatives name unrelated nodes or entirely different systems. | Nasopharyngeal lymph drainage
Tonsillar node | Which node region is particularly associated with palatine tonsillar drainage in standard teaching? | Jugulodigastric | Popliteal | Superficial inguinal | Axillary apex only | Jugulodigastric is a key upper deep cervical/tonsillar association. | The other nodal sites are anatomically distant from the stated principal drainage route. | Oropharyngeal lymph drainage
Velopharyngeal function | During swallowing, soft-palate/pharyngeal closure principally helps limit escape toward which route? | Nasal passage | Mandibular canal | External acoustic meatus through the eardrum | Lingual artery lumen | Velopharyngeal separation limits inappropriate oral-nasal communication. | The alternatives are not the normal bolus escape route addressed by this closure. | Velopharyngeal closure
Muscle groups | Which distinction correctly compares constrictors with longitudinal pharyngeal muscles? | Constrictors narrow the pharynx; longitudinal muscles contribute elevation/shortening | Both groups function only as nasal cartilages | All act solely to protrude the mandible | Longitudinal muscles have no swallowing role | Their actions are coordinated but usefully differentiated by predominant effects. | The alternatives deny muscular function or assign an unrelated action. | Longitudinal pharyngeal muscles
Voice taxonomy | A slide groups all nodules and polyps with malignant disease. Which correction is most appropriate? | Many such lesions are benign; hoarseness does not establish malignancy | Every nodule is cancer by definition | Benign lesions cannot affect voice vibration | The pharyngeal mucosa alone generates all voiced sound | Structural voice lesions require clinical interpretation, and benign lesions can disturb vocal-fold vibration. | The alternatives confuse pathology categories or phonation anatomy. | Benign vocal-fold lesions
Hoarseness inference | A teacher has persistent hoarseness. What follows from an anatomy differential list alone? | Several possible causes remain; appropriate evaluation is needed | Reflux is confirmed | A recurrent laryngeal nerve lesion is confirmed | A pharyngeal tumor is confirmed | A symptom and a list of possible mechanisms do not identify a single cause. | Each alternative overdiagnoses without the needed history and examination. | Voice differential categories
Autonomic qualification | Why is the slide label parasympathetic through VII incomplete as a universal pharyngeal rule? | Pathways depend on target glands and regions | VII supplies all skeletal motor function throughout pharynx and tongue | Autonomic fibers are identical to lymph vessels | All pharyngeal autonomic fibers are olfactory filaments | A regional secretomotor shorthand cannot explain every tissue and function. | The alternatives conflate motor, autonomic, lymphatic and olfactory systems. | Parasympathetic regional qualification
Model versus diagnosis | A diagram shows hypopharyngeal lymph routes. Which use is appropriate? | Learn regional routes while recognizing that individual disease spread needs assessment | Infer a specific cancer stage from the diagram alone | Assume every route is strictly unilateral without variation | Replace clinical examination with the printed arrows | The map supports anatomical understanding, not an individualized disease conclusion. | The alternatives overinterpret a generalized teaching diagram. | Hypopharyngeal lymph drainage
''')
cases(6, '''
Motor exception | Which tongue-associated muscle is the usual exception to XII motor supply? | Palatoglossus | Genioglossus | Hyoglossus | Superior longitudinal | Palatoglossus receives vagal motor supply through pharyngeal pathways in standard teaching. | The other muscles follow the hypoglossal motor rule. | Hypoglossal motor rule
Touch versus taste | Most anterior tongue touch and taste originate through which cranial pathways, respectively? | V3 and VII | VII and V3 | XII and IX | I and VIII | General sensation follows lingual V3; taste follows chorda tympani VII for most anterior mucosa. | The reversed pair swaps modalities; the other nerves do not provide these principal pathways. | Anterior tongue general sensation
Traveling fibers | Taste fibers travel with the lingual nerve. Which conclusion is correct? | Their cranial origin remains VII | They become V3-origin taste fibers | They become XII motor fibers | They lose their sensory role entirely | A shared carrier route does not change the origin or modality of the fibers. | The alternatives conflate route, parent nerve and function. | Anterior tongue taste
Vallate exception | Papillae just anterior to the sulcus terminalis receive taste principally through which nerve? | IX | VII without exception | XII | V1 | Circumvallate papillae are a key glossopharyngeal exception to a simple anterior VII map. | The alternatives ignore the exception or assign motor/other sensory territories. | Circumvallate papillae
Mechanical papillae | Which papilla type lacks taste buds in the standard classification? | Filiform | Fungiform | Circumvallate | Foliate | Filiform papillae have an important mechanical role. | The other three are associated with taste buds. | Filiform papillae
Shape control | Which intrinsic fibers principally narrow and elongate the tongue? | Transverse | Vertical | Genioglossus as an intrinsic muscle | Styloglossus as an intrinsic muscle | Transverse intrinsic fibers produce the stated shape contribution. | Vertical fibers flatten/broaden, while genioglossus and styloglossus are extrinsic. | Transverse tongue muscle
Extrinsic movement | Which muscle arises from the mandibular mental-spine region and contributes importantly to protrusion? | Genioglossus | Styloglossus | Temporalis | Masseter | The attachment and tongue-protruding role identify genioglossus. | Styloglossus retracts/elevates tongue; temporalis and masseter principally act on the jaw. | Genioglossus
Peripheral lesion | Assume an isolated right lower motor neuron XII lesion. Which protrusion pattern is expected? | Tongue deviates toward the right weak side | Tongue always deviates left for every lesion type | Taste from every tongue region disappears as the defining finding | The mandible must dislocate anteriorly | The stated peripheral motor context supports ipsilateral weakness/deviation. | The alternatives overgeneralize central/peripheral patterns or conflate motor tongue, taste and TMJ findings. | Peripheral XII lesion pattern
Surface landmark | Which landmark separates the oral and pharyngeal tongue parts on the dorsum? | Sulcus terminalis | Lingual frenulum | Mandibular notch | Inferior nasal meatus | The V-shaped groove is the terminal sulcus. | The frenulum is ventral, the notch is mandibular and the meatus is nasal. | Sulcus terminalis
Developmental shorthand | Which interpretation corrects the phrase tongue derives from arches one to four? | Contributions differ by region, second-arch tissue is largely overgrown, and muscles have a different developmental origin | All four arches contribute equally to every adult region | The phrase establishes XII taste supply | The tongue has no relation to development of mucosal sensory territories | The broad phrase needs regional and tissue-specific qualification. | Equal contributions, a taste role for XII and denial of developmental relations are incorrect. | Tongue developmental regions
Arterial origin | The lingual artery is principally a branch of which artery? | External carotid | Internal carotid | Anterior cerebral | Pulmonary | The lingual arterial route belongs to the external carotid system. | The alternatives do not provide its normal principal origin. | Lingual artery
Taste zones | Which statement about a rigid sweet-only tongue-tip map is most accurate? | It incorrectly makes taste territories mutually exclusive | It proves that only the tip contains any taste buds | It is required by the XII motor map | It follows from the absence of taste buds in fungiform papillae | Regional sensitivity differences do not imply exclusive quality zones. | Taste buds occur beyond the tip; XII is motor; fungiform papillae do have taste buds. | Taste-map qualification
Landmark sequence | Which standard adult sequence correctly matches oesophageal beginning, hiatus and approximate gastric entry? | C6, T10, T11 | T10, C6, T11 | T11, T3, C1 | C1, T11, T6 | These are useful approximate landmarks for different boundaries. | The alternatives reverse or misassign the anatomical levels. | Oesophageal hiatus
Normal narrowing | Why do some texts list three oesophageal constrictions while others list four? | Aortic and bronchial impressions may be grouped or separated | One scheme must describe a different organ | All normal narrowings are tumors | The oesophagus has no related external structures | Grouping conventions differ without necessarily contradicting the underlying anatomical impressions. | The alternatives mistake classification convention for a different organ or pathology. | Aortic and bronchial impressions
Bolus transport | What chiefly explains movement down the oesophagus beyond gravity alone? | Coordinated peristaltic activity | Contraction of nasal conchae | Taste buds pushing food through the wall | Movement of the mandibular condyle inside the oesophagus | Propagating muscular activity provides active transport. | The alternatives assign transport to unrelated or nonexistent mechanisms. | Peristalsis
Oral subphases | Chewing and saliva mixing followed by posterior tongue propulsion are best described as which sequence? | Oral preparation then oral propulsion | Oesophageal then nasal phase | Pharyngeal closure then taste generation only | Laryngeal phonation then coronary transport | The oral stage can be subdivided into preparation and transport. | The alternatives mix unrelated systems or omit the stated oral functions. | Oral preparatory phase
Airway protection | Which account best describes airway protection during swallowing? | Coordinated laryngeal closure, hyolaryngeal movement and associated events | A single epiglottic muscle guarantees safety independently | Soft-palate closure alone prevents all aspiration | One normal oral movement proves every later stage is safe | Protection is multifactorial and coordinated. | The epiglottis is cartilage, nasal separation is not equivalent to laryngeal protection, and one stage cannot establish all others. | Airway protection in swallowing
Segment opening | Which factors contribute to opening of the pharyngoesophageal segment? | Relaxation, hyolaryngeal traction and bolus-related forces | Rigid detachment of the cricoid from the skull | Taste from one papilla alone | Exclusive action of the external nasal cartilage | Neural and mechanical components cooperate in opening. | The alternatives describe nonexistent detachment or unrelated structures as sole mechanisms. | Pharyngoesophageal segment opening
Airway entry | Material passes below the vocal folds. Which term fits this defining boundary? | Aspiration | Penetration without aspiration | Nasal regurgitation | Normal oral preparation by definition | Passage below the vocal folds defines aspiration. | Penetration remains above that boundary; nasal regurgitation follows a different route; oral preparation does not define airway entry. | Penetration versus aspiration
Nasal escape | During a teaching swallow case, material escapes toward the nose. Which mechanism is most directly relevant to consider? | Incomplete velopharyngeal separation | Every instance of lower-airway aspiration | A torn TMJ disc as the only cause | A deficiency of tongue taste buds as the defining cause | Oral-nasal separation involves the soft palate and pharyngeal walls; the actual cause requires evaluation. | The alternatives equate different pathways or assign an unsupported exclusive cause. | Nasal regurgitation
''')
